{
  "image_name": "59b9d661612c80269fad17cc71f0a65b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several time-resolved measurement schemes for observing material dynamics after excitation. Overall, it contrasts continuous perturbation with pulsed excitation and shows how different probe strategies trade off time resolution, reversibility requirements, and whether the process can be followed as a movie or only at selected delays.\n\nAt the top, a continuously applied trigger is monitored by a fast camera, which records a sequence of delay windows over time. This setup appears suited to both reversible and irreversible behavior, with millisecond–second resolution.\n\nThe larger lower section focuses on pulsed excitation. In one approach, a single trigger pulse is followed by repeated fast-camera frames, capturing an evolving irreversible response over nanosecond to microsecond timescales. Another uses a single-shot probe delayed by Δt after one trigger, giving one snapshot of an irreversible event. A related “movie-mode” version gathers multiple delayed snapshots across the field of view, effectively reconstructing the transient sequence. Finally, a stroboscopic method repeats trigger–probe cycles at frequency 1/f with a short-pulse probe, allowing reversible dynamics to be sampled with much finer femtosecond to microsecond resolution.\n\nDashed decay curves indicate the transient response being sampled at different delays.",
